## 2. CMS Complaint Narrative (Self-Funded / Federal Jurisdiction)

**Filed via:** the CMS No Surprises complaint channel — **confirm the current portal and phone number at cms.gov/nosurprises before filing** (the 1-800-985-3059 number in our documents is unverified; consumer sources list 1-800-MEDICARE).

**Complaint against:** [Provider/Facility Name]; plan administrator identified as [Plan Name / Employer Name], a self-funded group health plan.

---

**Narrative:**

> I am submitting this complaint under the No Surprises Act regarding a suspected violation by an out-of-network provider [or facility].
>
> On [Date(s) of Service], I received [emergency care / non-emergency care from an out-of-network ancillary provider (anesthesiology/radiology/pathology) / other out-of-network care] at [Facility Name], an in-network facility under my [self-funded ERISA] plan. The provider has billed me $[Amount] beyond my in-network cost-sharing obligation, account #[Account Number].
>
> These services are protected under PHSA §§ 2799A-1 – 2799A-3 and 45 C.F.R. Part 149. My patient cost-sharing must equal the in-network amount. [Emergency/ancillary: No consent waiver can authorize this balance bill.] [Non-emergency OON: No valid notice-and-consent waiver satisfying 45 C.F.R. § 149.420 was timely provided.]
>
> On [date], I disputed the bill in writing via certified mail (USPS #[tracking #]) and notified my plan (ref #[#]). The provider placed a hold on collections (ref #[HLD-#]) but has not rescinded the balance or corrected the account to in-network cost-sharing as of [today's date].
>
> **Requested action:** I request that CMS (1) investigate this suspected violation of the No Surprises Act balance-billing prohibitions, (2) direct the provider to withdraw the balance beyond in-network cost-sharing and redirect any payment dispute to the Open Negotiation/IDR process under 45 C.F.R. §§ 149.500–149.510, and (3) confirm no adverse credit reporting occurs while this complaint is pending.
>
> I have made no partial payment or written acknowledgment of the disputed amount.

**Attachments:** same set as the PID complaint, plus plan documents identifying the plan as self-funded (SPD or plan administrator confirmation letter).

---

** File both simultaneously — one with the PID and one with CMS — once the plan type is confirmed. If it turns out to be fully insured, the PID version controls and the Act 146 external-review hook activates. If self-funded, the CMS version controls; the PID would then refer the complaint to the federal channel anyway, so filing both upfront loses nothing but risks being told to re-file — that's why confirming the funding type in writing with the plan administrator before filing is still the efficient order of operations.
